# **A key the menu does not use is ignored and does not stop the editor.**
#
# `MenuKey` dispatches over `KeyKind` with a `case` and no `otherwise`, which
# is the right shape -- 6.9.3.5 makes a selector no label matches a trap
# (ADR-0018), so a kind added later is a *reported* crash rather than a
# silently swallowed key. What it costs is that the arm listing the kinds the
# menu ignores has to be revisited whenever a kind is added, and ADR-0396 did
# not: `kkOpen` and `kkNextDoc` arrived with the second document, and
#
#   runtime error: case: no label matches the selector at apedit.pas:1509:9
#
# is what F3 or F6 did with the menu open in v3.10.0. Every oracle here was
# green, because no session had ever pressed one of those keys while a panel
# was up -- and `kind-exhaustive`, which exists for exactly this and answers
# it correctly when pointed at `tui/apedit.pas`, reads the compiler's own
# three components and nothing else.
#
# So this session presses, with the menu bar up and again with a drop-down
# open, every key `MenuKey` ignores that some *other* key of the editor is
# bound to: F3 (Open), F6 (Next document), Ctrl-S, Ctrl-B, Ctrl-F, Ctrl-L,
# Ctrl-G, Ctrl-Z, Ctrl-Y, Backspace, Delete, Home, End and an ordinary
# character. The claim is the whole of what the goldens show: the menu is
# still on screen and unmoved, and the editor is still running.
name m.pas
size 10 34
push program p(output);
push begin
push end.
draw
func 10
func 3
func 6
ctrl S
ctrl B
ctrl F
ctrl L
ctrl G
ctrl Z
ctrl Y
keys z
draw
esc B
esc B
func 3
func 6
ctrl H
esc 3~
esc H
esc F
keys z
draw
ctrl C
draw
